desc: Whale of a Time (Orca port) by chmaha
tags: midi generator sequencer livecoding
author: chmaha
version: 1.1
changelog:
  Add full copyright and licence header (upstream authors' notices).
about:
  Whale of a Time is a port of Orca, the esoteric livecoding sequencer by Hundred Rabbits. Every letter on the
  64 x 32 grid is an operator: it reads the cells beside it and writes its result below, once per frame. Clocks,
  delays, tracks and euclidean rhythms bang MIDI note operators (: and %), so a few characters make a pattern.
  Click a cell and type to edit, Shift+arrows or drag to select, Ctrl+C/X/V to copy, cut and paste,
  Ctrl+Z/Y to undo and redo, Delete or Backspace to erase. The hint bar explains the operator under the cursor;
  the ? button lists them all. Input ports light up teal, outputs orange, and operators sit on coloured tiles.
  The clock follows REAPER's transport (frames lock to the beat grid, Ticks per Beat sets their length) or runs
  free at its own tempo. What you see is what is saved, with the project and in REAPER presets.
  Patches change the grid as they run (movers fly, writers overwrite cells). With REWIND ON PLAY each start
  of playback puts the grid back to exactly what you typed, so every take is the same; turn it off to let
  the patch carry on evolving from where it stopped, like the original Orca.
  Drop a .orca text file on the grid to import it. EXAMPLES holds 37 patches, from a first beat and one demo
  per operator up to a sequencer, a tracker and a CC knob bank. Right-click the grid for edit options.
  New instances start on a blank canvas.
  If REAPER grabs keys you type, enable Send all keyboard input to plug-in in the FX window's + menu.
  Supported: A-Z, lowercase (bang-only) operators, *, #, : (note), % (mono note), ! (CC) and ? (pitch bend).
  OSC (=), UDP (;) and $ are shown but do nothing.
  Ported from Orca by Hundred Rabbits (Devine Lu Linvega and Rekka Bellum)
  https://github.com/hundredrabbits/Orca (MIT)
  Examples include the Orca-c example patches, https://github.com/hundredrabbits/Orca-c (MIT)
  JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license
  Claude generated some of this code
